doc:sec-11a a cof:Section ;
    cof:title "11A Telecommunications service warrant for collection of foreign" ;
    values:kind "section" ;
    values:originalText "intelligence  (1) Where:  (a) the Director-General of Security gives a notice in writing to the Attorney-General requesting the Attorney-General to issue a warrant under this section authorising persons approved under section 12 in respect of the warrant to do acts or things referred to in subsection 9(1) in relation to a particular telecommunications service for the purpose of obtaining foreign intelligence relating to a matter specified in the notice; and  Chapter 2  Interception of telecommunications" ;
    cof:pageNumber "125"^^xsd:integer .

concept:telecommunications-interception-and-access-act-1979-sec-11b a cml:Concept ;
    skos:prefLabel "11B Named person warrant for collection of foreign intelligence"@en ;
    cml:realizedBy doc:sec-11b ;
    values:originalText "(1) The Attorney-General may, under his or her hand, issue a warrant in respect of a person if:  (a) the Director-General of Security gives a notice in writing to the Attorney-General requesting the Attorney-General to issue a warrant under this section authorising persons approved under section 12 in respect of the warrant to do acts or things referred to in subsection 9A(1A) in relation to:  (i) communications that are being made to or from any telecommunications service that a person or foreign organisation is using, or is likely to use; or  (ii) communications that are being made by means of a particular telecommunications device or particular telecommunications devices that a person or foreign organisation is using, or is likely to use;  for the purpose of obtaining foreign intelligence relating to a matter specified in the notice; and  (b) the Attorney-General is satisfied, on the basis of advice received from the Minister for Defence or the Minister for Foreign Affairs, that:  (i) the obtaining of foreign intelligence relating to that matter is in the interests of Australia’s national security, Australia’s foreign relations or Australia’s national economic well-being; and  (ii) it is necessary to intercept the communications of the person or foreign organisation in order to obtain the intelligence referred to in paragraph (a); and  (iii) relying on a telecommunications service warrant to obtain the intelligence would be ineffective.  (1A) The warrant authorises persons approved under section 12 in respect of the warrant to intercept, subject to any conditions or restrictions that are specified in the warrant:  Chapter 2  Interception of telecommunications" ;
    skos:definition "(1) The Attorney-General may, under his or her hand, issue a warrant in respect of a person if:  (a) the Director-General of Security gives a notice in writing to the Attorney-General requesting the Attorney-General to issue a warrant under this section authorising persons approved under section 12 in respect of the warrant to do acts or things referred to in subsection 9A(1A) in relation to:  (i) communications that are being made to or from any telecommunications service that a person or foreign organisation is using, or is likely to use; or  (ii) communications that are being made by means of a particular telecommunications device or particular telecommunications devices that a person or foreign organisation is using, or is likely to use;  for the purpose of obtaining foreign intelligence relating to a matter specified in the notice; and  (b) the Attorney-General is satisfied, on the basis of advice received from the Minister for Defence or the Minister for Foreign Affairs, that:  (i) the obtaining of foreign intelligence relating to that matter is in the interests of Australia’s national security, Australia’s foreign relations or Australia’s national economic well-being; and  (ii) it is necessary to intercept the communications of the person or foreign organisation in order to obtain the intelligence referred to in paragraph (a); and  (iii) relying on a telecommunications service warrant to obtain the intelligence would be ineffective.  (1A) The warrant authorises persons approved under section 12 in respect of the warrant to intercept, subject to any conditions or restrictions that are specified in the warrant:  Chapter 2  Interception of telecommunications"@en ;
    cml:integrityHash "dd5ec6c52d97b8314f78c9c75767b7b6f98c68201cb17dfa043ce211e8485016" ;
    cof:pageNumber "127"^^xsd:integer ;
    cml:hasPart concept:telecommunications-interception-and-access-act-1979-sec-11b-ss-1 ;
    cml:hasPart concept:telecommunications-interception-and-access-act-1979-sec-11b-ss-1a ;
    cml:curationStatus cml:Proposed ;
    cml:schemaVersion "2" ;
    cml:proposedBy <https://ns.webcivics.net/tools/legis2cml> ;
    prov:wasDerivedFrom <telecommunications-interception-and-access-act-1979.pdf> ;
    dc:source "telecommunications-interception-and-access-act-1979.pdf, p.127" .
doc:sec-11b a cof:Section ;
    cof:title "11B Named person warrant for collection of foreign intelligence" ;
    values:kind "section" ;
    values:originalText "(1) The Attorney-General may, under his or her hand, issue a warrant in respect of a person if:  (a) the Director-General of Security gives a notice in writing to the Attorney-General requesting the Attorney-General to issue a warrant under this section authorising persons approved under section 12 in respect of the warrant to do acts or things referred to in subsection 9A(1A) in relation to:  (i) communications that are being made to or from any telecommunications service that a person or foreign organisation is using, or is likely to use; or  (ii) communications that are being made by means of a particular telecommunications device or particular telecommunications devices that a person or foreign organisation is using, or is likely to use;  for the purpose of obtaining foreign intelligence relating to a matter specified in the notice; and  (b) the Attorney-General is satisfied, on the basis of advice received from the Minister for Defence or the Minister for Foreign Affairs, that:  (i) the obtaining of foreign intelligence relating to that matter is in the interests of Australia’s national security, Australia’s foreign relations or Australia’s national economic well-being; and  (ii) it is necessary to intercept the communications of the person or foreign organisation in order to obtain the intelligence referred to in paragraph (a); and  (iii) relying on a telecommunications service warrant to obtain the intelligence would be ineffective.  (1A) The warrant authorises persons approved under section 12 in respect of the warrant to intercept, subject to any conditions or restrictions that are specified in the warrant:  Chapter 2  Interception of telecommunications" ;
    cof:pageNumber "127"^^xsd:integer .

