concept:telecommunications-interception-and-access-act-1979-sec-180 a cml:Concept ;
    skos:prefLabel "180 Authorisations for access to prospective information or"@en ;
    cml:realizedBy doc:sec-180 ;
    values:originalText "documents  (1) Sections 276, 277 and 278 of the Telecommunications Act 1997 do not prevent a disclosure of information or a document if the information or document is covered by an authorisation in force under this section. Prospective authorisation  (2) An authorised officer of a criminal law-enforcement agency may authorise the disclosure of specified information or specified documents that come into existence during the period for which the authorisation is in force. Authorisation for access to existing information or documents may also be sought  (3) The authorised officer may, in that authorisation, also authorise the disclosure of specified information or specified documents that came into existence before the time the authorisation comes into force. Limits on making the authorisation  (4) The authorised officer must not make the authorisation unless he or she is satisfied that the disclosure is reasonably necessary for:  (a) in any case—the investigation of a serious offence; or  (b) in any case—the investigation of an offence against a law of the Commonwealth, a State or a Territory that is punishable by imprisonment for at least 3 years; or  (c) in the case of an authorised officer of the ACC—the performance of the ACC’s function covered by  Chapter 4  Access to telecommunications data" ;
    skos:definition "documents  (1) Sections 276, 277 and 278 of the Telecommunications Act 1997 do not prevent a disclosure of information or a document if the information or document is covered by an authorisation in force under this section. Prospective authorisation  (2) An authorised officer of a criminal law-enforcement agency may authorise the disclosure of specified information or specified documents that come into existence during the period for which the authorisation is in force. Authorisation for access to existing information or documents may also be sought  (3) The authorised officer may, in that authorisation, also authorise the disclosure of specified information or specified documents that came into existence before the time the authorisation comes into force. Limits on making the authorisation  (4) The authorised officer must not make the authorisation unless he or she is satisfied that the disclosure is reasonably necessary for:  (a) in any case—the investigation of a serious offence; or  (b) in any case—the investigation of an offence against a law of the Commonwealth, a State or a Territory that is punishable by imprisonment for at least 3 years; or  (c) in the case of an authorised officer of the ACC—the performance of the ACC’s function covered by  Chapter 4  Access to telecommunications data"@en ;
    cml:integrityHash "9b16b44d0e0a5cac4f968befb0b5b5eba913f9ef3dfa1501ed728b6ead72a357" ;
    cof:pageNumber "375"^^xsd:integer ;
    cml:hasPart concept:telecommunications-interception-and-access-act-1979-sec-180-ss-1 ;
